## Lore

Wingrider Pilots fly the Wingrider Flying Wing, an open flying platform the
pilot rides in power armor rather than sits inside. They are the Warlords''
couriers, scouts, patrol riders and air cavalry, and they have a reputation as
arrogant daredevils who treat incoming fire as part of the game.

What sets them apart is how hard they work the machine: standing on the wing,
hanging from the stirrups, steering with their feet, diving, rolling and
whipping around cliffs and trees on a hydraulic grapnel. A new pilot is not
accepted until he has flown with his feet at least once.

Warlord Kolodenko introduced the Wingrider about twenty years ago and still
fields the largest and most seasoned force. Copies have since reached other
Warlords and the Black Market, so pilots can now be found among mercenaries
and bandits as well, which Kolodenko''s riders take as a personal insult.

## GM Notes

Vehicle: a Wingrider Flying Wing with its corresponding Wingrider power armor
and jet pack, all described in the book''s Vehicle Section. They are not on the
equipment list.

When piloting a Flying Wing or another aerial or rocket vehicle: +1
action/attack per melee, +1 to strike with the vehicle, +1 on initiative, and
+2 to dodge on a small vehicle (hovercycle, motorcycle, snowmobile). These
stack with the class''s flat bonuses.

The book prints "+2 to save vs punch, fall or impact". It is stored as
printed; it most likely means +2 to roll with punch, fall or impact.

Related Pilot skills get +10%, but +15% for hovercycles and aircraft; the
sheet applies the +10% only.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: an energy pistol and an energy rifle
of choice, one other weapon of choice, a CD player with 2D6 discs, two rocket
flares, two uniforms denoting War Camp and Warlord, casual clothing and a
handful of personal items. Money is 1D6x1000 credits; pay is about 1,500
credits a month plus a small percentage of booty, with the Flying Wing, power
armor, other equipment and general needs provided by the War Camp.

On assignment the pilot may be given different power armor and vehicles,
additional or different weapons (including fusion blocks) and other mission
gear. The save vs Horror Factor rises by +1 at levels 3, 6, 8, 10, 12 and 14.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'wingrider-rpa-pilot');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
